WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:19.350
I'm Dr. Mark Colquitt. I'm a bariatric surgeon at the Fort Sanders Center for Bariatric Surgery.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.899
It's located at Fort Sanders Regional Medical Center. My practice is Foothills Weight Loss Specialists and

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:31.529
Premier Surgical Associates. We often recommend

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:38.279
robotic bypass surgery for our patients who have diabetes and other severe

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.070
ailments of severe obesity.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.680
We have an animated video

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.860
available that we'd like to show you now. It's meant to educate the patients and the families.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.949
If you suffer from severe obesity

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:59.219
and have been unable to lose weight, we may recommend robotic gastric bypass surgery.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.189
Your digestive system or gastrointestinal tract includes your mouth,

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.490
esophagus,

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.680
stomach, small

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:14.129
Intestine and large intestine.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.140
Your pancreas, liver

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.390
and gallbladder

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.959
squirt digestive juices into your small intestine to help break down the food you eat.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:38.039
We may recommend bariatric surgery if you're unable to lose weight and keep it off through diet and exercise

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.530
alone, have a body mass index,

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:48.390
or BMI, above 40, have a BMI above 35 and you have a life-threatening condition

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:51.359
such as heart disease or diabetes, or

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:56.910
weigh more than 100 pounds over your ideal body weight or IBW.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.340
Gastric bypass surgery

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:08.040
helps you lose weight by bypassing your stomach and some of your small intestine to redirect the path of the food through your digestive system.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:15.059
This stimulates various digestion hormones that attach themselves to

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.330
receptors in your brain and result in

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.419
decreasing your appetite and burning more calories.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.459
Before your procedure an intravenous line will be started. This allows

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:33.899
antibiotics to be given through the IV to decrease your chance of infection.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.209
Also, gentle anesthesia medications will be given through this IV. A

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:45.660
breathing tube will be inserted through your mouth and down your throat to help you breathe during the operation. I

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.890
will make a small incision near your belly button and

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.220
insert a plastic tube called a port.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.799
Carbon dioxide gas will be pumped into your abdomen through this port.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:09.060
The gas will inflate your abdomen giving us more room to see and move the surgical tools.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:17.159
After your abdomen is inflated a high-definition camera will be inserted into this port. I

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:26.669
will make additional port incisions for robotic instruments as well as for instruments used by surgical assistants.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.329
An assistant will insert all the robotic tools through this port.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:40.229
unlike standard laparoscopic instruments, these tools can rotate 360 degrees and have more

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:42.569
flexibility than the human wrist.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.140
Seated in a special console, I

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.799
will operate the robotic arms and the camera with joystick-like controls and foot pedals.

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:03.689
A computer will translate the exact movements of my fingers into precise movements of these surgical tools.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:07.379
At the same time a

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.389
high-definition vision system will provide a magnified three-dimensional

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:17.940
stereoscopic view of the surgical area to perform the gastric bypass procedure.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:22.978
We will divide the stomach creating a small upper portion called a pouch.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:27.209
This remaining lower portion of the stomach remains in place.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:35.459
The small intestine will be then cut and the lower section of your small intestine will be attached to the stomach pouch.

00:04:36.699 --> 00:04:43.829
Then I reattach the upper section of your small intestine to a different part of your lower small intestine,

00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:49.799
which will allow digestive juices from your pancreas, liver, and gallbladder to help digest your food.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:57.179
At the end of the procedure the tiny incisions will be closed with stitches and surgical glue.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:07.120
After your procedure your breathing tube will be removed and you will be taken to the recovery room for monitoring.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.579
You'll be given pain medication as needed.

00:05:10.159 --> 00:05:13.419
You may continue receiving antibiotics through your IV.

00:05:14.539 --> 00:05:18.218
Most patients are released from the hospital by one to two days.

